WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.030
Let's shutter those windows, remove our blindfolds,
and take a good look at the film that caused

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:08.120
so much hype over the 2018 holiday season.

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:10.760
This is the untold truth of Bird Box.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.040
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:13.049 --> 00:00:16.960
While it may have felt like someone simply
took The Happening and A Quiet Place and smooshed

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:22.000
them together into a new film called Bird
Box, the director, Susanne Bier, has confirmed

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.300
that wasn't the case at all.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:24.480
She told Polygon,

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:25.480
"No, no, no.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:29.490
We were shooting [in April 2018] when we had
heard of A Quiet Place and we were almost

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.000
finished editing when it came out."

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:35.960
The film is actually based on the novel of
the same name, published by Josh Malerman

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:36.920
in 2014.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.180
One major difference between the novel and
the film is the ending.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:44.520
In the book, when Malorie, Boy, and Girl all
reach the sanctuary at the end of the river,

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:48.719
it doesn't turn out to be quite the paradise
they were expecting, considering the other

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.000
survivors have purposefully blinded themselves
for protection.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:56.680
This change from a deflated, discouraging
end to a slightly more hopeful finale was

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:59.280
explained by director Bier, who pointed out,

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:04.000
"I'm not particularly interested for the audience
to leave, from the cinema or their own screen,

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.040
with a kind of completely bleak point of view."

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:11.200
One of the other larger deviations from the
original text came in the form of some significant

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.380
changes to a handful of the characters.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:18.300
In the first act of the film, Malorie's sister
Jessica is quickly whisked away after she

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.400
sees a creature while driving Malorie home.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:24.760
In the book, though, she survives along with
her sister for a few months before falling

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.140
victim to the monsters.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.880
Malorie's love interest, Tom, was also altered.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:33.160
Rather than surviving in the house and going
on to raise Boy and Girl with Malorie, he

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:36.140
falls victim to Gary while she is busy delivering
her baby.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:37.420
And then there's Douglas.

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:42.280
While he ended up being a crucial part of
the movie, his character was entirely non-existent

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:46.720
in the book, although the novel's character
Dom seems to closely fit that role.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.320
From Christian Bale's shocking transformation
for The Machinist to Colin Firth's preparation

00:01:52.329 --> 00:01:57.200
for The King's Speech, the way an actor preps
for a role can be fascinating, and Bird Box

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:58.240
is no exception.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:02.180
Bullock did some intense research before shooting
her blindfold scenes.

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:06.870
According to Mirror, Bier revealed that Bullock
spent time with a blind man who specializes

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.060
in teaching other blind people to navigate.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:13.520
At one point, Bullock's commitment hurt her
on set, when her blindfold led her into a

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.860
collision with a camera that drew blood.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:19.780
While she may have been committed to the role
regardless of her own safety, Bullock's passion

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:23.300
for authenticity did give way when it came
to the safety of others.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:27.600
She had the blindfold reduced to a single
layer of thickness whenever she had to carry

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.320
her onscreen kids through a scene.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.980
"There was always an element of me adhered
to those kids to make sure that I knew where

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:34.480
they were.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.520
I needed to return them to their parents at
the end of the day, that was my goal."

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:43.260
Bullock trained to be able to move without
sight in Bird Box, but she purposefully didn't

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.980
prepare for another aspect of her character's
journey.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.780
The first time she stepped into the boat that
takes her and the kids down the river, she

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:51.540
had no boating experience.

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:56.160
Bullock considered the story carefully and
decided to use her aquatic ignorance in her

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.620
favor, insisting that the river scenes be
filmed in chronological order so that her

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:04.620
rowing could look better as the movie progressed,
precisely as it would look for the equally

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:06.060
inexperienced Malorie.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:11.810
At the end of Bird Box's first week, the Netflix
Twitter account posted a tweet boasting that

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:17.410
the movie had already been watched by an impressive
45 million accounts, giving it the "best first

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:19.420
7 days ever for a Netflix film!"

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:24.100
While that's a milestone worth celebrating,
many news outlets were quick to criticize

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:26.680
the figure as less meaningful than it seemed.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.380
Netflix's tight control of its viewership
numbers has made any stat shared by the company

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:36.100
less trustworthy than the ratings system used
for cable and broadcast networks.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:40.200
Did 45 million people really stream Bird Box
in its first week?

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:44.110
Maybe not, but the film's buzz certainly makes
it easy to believe that everyone and their

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:47.500
mother was watching this thing over the 2018
holiday break.

